WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.785 --> 00:00:01.898
(gasps)

00:00:01.898 --> 00:00:03.670
- What is that?

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.896
(crying softly)

00:00:05.896 --> 00:00:08.479
(uneasy music)

00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:17.180
- [Daniel Myrick] One of the
best ways to watch the film

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.770
is alone on a crappy VHS
or some old DVD player.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.840
Ed and I both knew that we wanted the film

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.963
to feel like a documentary.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.700
We shot the film chronologically.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.500
We had way points guided by GPS

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.880
so they could navigate through the woods

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.050
without being kind of hindered by a crew.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.080
We shadowed them as they were
moving through the trees.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.170
- [Eduardo Sanchez] Dan
and I kept away from them

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.270
as much as possible.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.750
- [Dan] And there would be a bicycle flag,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.020
so when they got into the
general area of their way point

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.970
that's on their GPS,
they could see the flag.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.120
So that's when they were
instructed to pitch a tent.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.360
This is where you're
camping for the night.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.330
We had those plastic milk crates,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.310
and inside would be food stuffs,

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.840
fresh batteries for the cameras,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.220
film for the film camera,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.120
and then there would be these
old school film canisters,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.360
and inside those little plastic containers

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.590
would be directing notes.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.130
And they were all instructed
to not read each other's notes.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.230
So we set up this
conflict, but we allow them

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.230
to choose the time when
they want to have it.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.350
The mandate was just
to roll on everything.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.750
The name of the scene in the
script, The Confessional,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.000
it was the moment in the film

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.390
where it stopped being a
project about the Blair Witch

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.223
and started being a chronicling
of their last days on Earth.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.640
- [Eduardo] We were directing the actors

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.630
like their conscience speaking to them.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.847
For Heather, it was like,
"You're responsible for this.

00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:36.967
"Say your goodbyes.

00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:40.617
"Turn the camera light on and
point the camera at your face.

00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:42.460
"Just give us everything you've got."

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.010
- [Dan] She took the camera
and walked off by herself

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.873
and just had this monologue
to end all monologues.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.950
- There was a lot more snot.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.913
There was rivers and rivers of snot.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.950
We wanted to strike a balance
between the performance

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.880
and not getting too
distracted with the snot flow.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.080
- [Eduardo] But I remember
first seeing that monologue,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.300
and our jaws dropped.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.167
And I think that was the
moment where we were like,

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:08.690
"Wow, this really could work
if we don't screw it up."

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.510
We knew it was something special.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.839
And we kind of made this
little bit of film history.

00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:16.303
(intense music)

